  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn anomalous upper level ridge advancing into central sections of \nfar northern Canada will open the door to the rapid amplification of \nan Omega Block over the central Contiguous United States for the end \nof the work week. Upper level high pressure will then stall out and \nimpact the forecast area through Monday of next week. Quite the \nstructure with H3 heights reaching 588 dam over portions of the \nLower Peninsula this weekend and +13C 850mb temperatures advecting \nwell into Hudson Bay. Ideal mid Autumn conditions with high \ntemperatures reaching the middle to upper 60s, 5 to 10 degrees above \nnormal, and lows falling into the 40s.\n\nThe only weather of significance the next few days will be br and fog \nchances during the daybreak periods. A better setup for fog will be \nFriday morning with onshore flow advecting a marine airmass inland. \nThe relatively warm lakes will offer higher dewpoints that combined \nwith weak flow will provide for saturation potential. Surface \ncondensation pressure deficits support fog beginning in the tip of \nthe Thumb tonight before expanding southeastward through all of the \narea. The fog potential for Saturday morning looks to be the \ngreatest near Lake Erie before expanding westward. Could see a \nscenario where the Detroit urban heat islands remain just warm \nenough to dissuade the fog formation. Persistent light easterly wind \nshould bring more fog chances Sunday morning. Will likely add areas \nof fog in the forecast for tonight/Friday morning and maintain the \npatchy mention this weekend.\n\nModels suggest the amplitude of the omega will shear off or flatten \nby Monday. Indications are that a weak cold front could push \nsouthward invof Southeast Michigan, however, current data suggests a \ndominance of anticyclonic flow trajectories that will not favor \nlarge scale synoptic ascent. Preference is for a dry forecast through \nnext Wednesday.",
